<section xml:id="section-groups-properties">
  <title>First Properties of Groups</title>
  <p>
    Having defined a group and given a decent number of examples, I
    can now move back to developing the general theory. This sections
    has a number of useful definitions as well as a number of basic
    proposition that help to understand how a group behaves. I'll
    start with some direct clarifications of the group axioms
    themselves. 
  </p>
  <p>
    When talking about groups in general, it is pretty usual to write
    the binary operation as a multiplication and talk about products.
    In specific settings, of course, this might not be the case, but
    we have adopted these choices for the general case. Just remember
    that writing <m>ab</m> or talking about <sq>multiplication</sq>
    and <sq>product</sq> might mean something very different if I went
    to an example. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The identity element of a group is unique. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Assume that both <m>e</m> and <m>f</m> are identity elements
        of a group <m>G</m>. The multiplication by either has no
        effect: this is the definition of an identity. This
        observation allows me to write the following equation.
        <me>
          e = ef = f 
        </me>
          THe first equality recognizes that multiplication by
          <m>f</m> has not effect, and the second equality recognizes
          that multiplication by <m>e</m> has no effect. This proves
          the claim: the two identities must, in fact, be the same
          group element. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-unique-inverses">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Inverse elements in groups are unique. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m>g \in G</m> be a group element. Assume that both
        <m>h</m> and <m>k</m> are inverse elements. By definition,
        this means that multiplying <m>g</m> by either (in either
        order) produces the identity. Using this fact, I can write the
        following equation. 
        <me>
          h = h \Id = h(gk) = (hg)k = \Id k = k 
        </me>
        I'm using the assumption of the proof (that both of these are
        inverses). I'm also using the associativity of the binary
        operation in the middle step. I conclude that the two inverses
        must be the same group element, so inverses are also unique. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    In the next propositions, I want to talk about algebra of groups,
    in the high-school sense of <sq>algebra</sq>: solving equations
    and using variables. Since groups have a binary operation,
    algebraic equations and variables make sense. However, groups can
    consist of many different kinds of mathematical objects. It's not
    clear, at the start, what parts of algebra still work. Therefore,
    we should re-establish what rules still hold. One of the
    challenges of abstraction is realizing that the old rules you are
    used to may, in fact, fail in new settings. Thankfully, the
    following propositions show that some algebraic manipulation in
    groups still works. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The cancellation law holds in groups. Specifically, consider
        this equation of elements of some group. 
        <me>
          gh = gk
        </me>
        Then I can cancel <m>g</m> and conclude that <m>h = k</m>. The
        same is true for the following equation.
        <me>
          hg = kg
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof here is pretty straightforward: just multiply on the
        left by the inverse and then reduce <m>g^{-1}g</m> to the
        identify (which is the definition of an inverse). 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            gh = gk 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            g^{-1}gh = g^{-1}gk 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \Id h = \Id k 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            h = k 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The proof for the second equation is precisely the same steps
        just multiplying by the inverse on the right. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    So cancellation works, with is great, since it is a very natural
    and instinctual operation. However, care is still required here:
    cancellation only works if the cancelled element is on the same
    side of both product. Consider this equation of elements of some
    group. 
    <me>
      gh = kg
    </me>
    In general, this <m>g</m> cannot be cancelled. For an example,
    consider this equation in <m>D_4</m>.
    <me>
      F_3 R_3 = R_1 F_3 
    </me>
    From <xref ref="table-d4-multiplication"/>, I can check that both
    of these product are equal fo <m>F_4</m>. However, if I could
    cancel <m>F_3</m>, I would produce the false equality <m>R_3 =
    R_3</m>. So care is required for non-abelian groups and
    cancellation. (If the group is abelian, of course,
    then I can change the order of equations and this cancellation
    would be fine.) 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g,h \in G</m> and let <m>x</m> be a variable that
        stands for an unknown element of the same group. Then the
        equation
        <me>
          gx = h
        </me>
        always has a unique solution in the group <m>G</m>. Likewise,
        the equation 
        <me>
          xg = h
        </me>
        also has a unique solution. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof here is, again, just multiplication by
        <m>g^{-1}</m>. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            gx = h 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            g^{-1}gx = g^{-1}h}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            x = g^{-1}h}
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The equation is solve by <m>g^{-1}h</m>. Since inverses are
        unique by <xref ref="proposition-unique-inverses"/>, this
        solution to the equation is a unique solution. The second
        equation is solve in the same manner, just multiply on the
        right instead of on the left. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Let me give a couple of propositions for working with inverses. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g \in G</m>. Then <m>(g^{-1})^{-1} = g</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        This proposition say that the inverse of <m>g^{-1}</m> is g. I
        alreay know that the inverse of <m>g</m> is <m>g^{-1}</m>,
        which, by definition, means the following. 
        <me>
         g g^{-1} = g^{-1}g = \Id 
        </me>
        However, the same equation reinterpreted mean exactly that
        <m>g</m> is the inverse of <m>g^{-1}</m>, which proves the
        claim. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g,h \in G</m>. Then <m>(gh)^{-1} = h^{-1}
        g^{-1}</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        To prove this, I have to prove that <m>h^{-1}g^{-1}</m> is the
        inverse of <m>gh</m>, which means I have to show that the
        multiplication by the potential inverse, on both sides,
        produces the identity. I'll first do multiplication on the
        left. 
        <me>
          h^{-1}g^{-1}gh = h^{-1}\Id h = h^{-1}h = \Id 
        </me>
        Similarly, I can do multiplication on the right.
        <me>
          ghh^{-1}g^{-1} = g \Id g^{-1} = gg^{-1} = \Id 
        </me>
        In both equation, I have used the fact that the inverses of
        <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> produce the identity. Both equations
        together justify the proposition. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
</section>
